  "body": "```\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<script>\nvar td = document.createElement(\"td\");\ntd.setAttribute(\"colspan\", \"0\");\ntd.setAttribute(\"rowspan\", \"0\");\nvar s = td.colSpan + \" \" + td.rowSpan;\ntd.setAttribute(\"colspan\", \"2147483647\");\ntd.setAttribute(\"rowspan\", \"2147483647\");\ndocument.documentElement.textContent = s + \" \" + td.colSpan + \" \" + td.rowSpan;\n</script>\n```\n\nSpec seems to say: 0 0 2147483647 2147483647\nFirefox: 1 0 1 65534\nChrome: 1 1 8190 65534\nIE 11: 1 1 2147483647\n\n(I still haven't gotten Edge or WebKit working on my machines, so someone please post the results for those.)\n\nUpshot:\n- All browsers change a .colSpan of 0 to 1.  This should presumably be specced.\n- Chrome and IE 11 change a .rowSpan of 0 to 1 as well, but not Firefox.  Since the spec allows a rowspan of 0, Chrome and IE are just wrong.\n- All three browsers either clamp a large rowspan to 65534 or treat it as invalid.  This is probably not worth speccing.\n- Firefox and Chrome both clamp a large colspan or treat as invalid, but IE does not.  We should probably keep the spec like IE.\n\nConclusion: add a special provision to the spec that getting .colSpan returns 1 instead of 0.  Agreed?\n",
